{"subjectId":"sql","id":"duplicates","title":"Finding & Deleting Duplicates","group":"Interview Prep","order":56,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Find rows that repeat a value that should be unique, show all copies, and delete all but one — three variations of the same <code>GROUP BY</code>/<code>ROW_NUMBER</code> idea.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT * FROM contacts ORDER BY id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>id | name  | email\n---+-------+---------------\n1  | Asha  | asha@mail.com\n2  | Ravi  | ravi@mail.com\n3  | Asha  | ASHA@mail.com\n4  | Meera | meera@mail.com\n5  | Ravi  | ravi@mail.com\n6  | Ravi  | ravi@mail.com</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Which values are duplicated?</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT email, COUNT(*) AS copies\nFROM contacts\nGROUP BY email\nHAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1;\n\n-- case-insensitive duplicates\nSELECT lower(email) AS email, COUNT(*) AS copies, group_concat(id) AS ids\nFROM contacts\nGROUP BY lower(email)\nHAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1\nORDER BY email;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>email         | copies\n--------------+-------\nravi@mail.com | 3\n\nemail         | copies | ids\n--------------+--------+------\nasha@mail.com | 2      | 1,3\nravi@mail.com | 3      | 2,5,6</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-details\"></i>Show every duplicate row</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT id, name, email,\n       COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY email) AS copies\nFROM contacts\nORDER BY email, id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>id | name  | email          | copies\n---+-------+----------------+-------\n3  | Asha  | ASHA@mail.com  | 1\n1  | Asha  | asha@mail.com  | 1\n4  | Meera | meera@mail.com | 1\n2  | Ravi  | ravi@mail.com  | 3\n5  | Ravi  | ravi@mail.com  | 3\n6  | Ravi  | ravi@mail.com  | 3</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- rows whose (name, email) combination appears more than once\nSELECT c.*\nFROM contacts c\nJOIN (SELECT name, email FROM contacts GROUP BY name, email HAVING COUNT(*) &gt; 1) d\n  ON d.name = c.name AND d.email = c.email\nORDER BY c.id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>id | name | email\n---+------+--------------\n2  | Ravi | ravi@mail.com\n5  | Ravi | ravi@mail.com\n6  | Ravi | ravi@mail.com</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trash\"></i>Delete duplicates, keep the lowest id</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>DELETE FROM contacts\nWHERE id IN (\n    SELECT id FROM (\n        SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY email ORDER BY id) AS rn\n        FROM contacts\n    ) WHERE rn &gt; 1\n);\n\nSELECT * FROM contacts ORDER BY id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>id | name  | email\n---+-------+---------------\n1  | Asha  | asha@mail.com\n2  | Ravi  | ravi@mail.com\n3  | Asha  | ASHA@mail.com\n4  | Meera | meera@mail.com</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- without window functions: keep MIN(id) of each group\nDELETE FROM contacts\nWHERE id NOT IN (SELECT MIN(id) FROM contacts GROUP BY email);\n\nSELECT * FROM contacts ORDER BY id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>id | name  | email\n---+-------+---------------\n1  | Asha  | asha@mail.com\n2  | Ravi  | ravi@mail.com\n3  | Asha  | ASHA@mail.com\n4  | Meera | meera@mail.com</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Contact 3 (<code>ASHA@mail.com</code>) survived both deletes: the comparison is case-sensitive. Partition/group by <code>lower(email)</code> when duplicates should be detected case-insensitively.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>MySQL / SQL Server</figcaption><pre><code>-- MySQL: self-join delete\nDELETE c1 FROM contacts c1\nJOIN contacts c2 ON c2.email = c1.email AND c2.id &lt; c1.id;\n\n-- SQL Server: delete through a CTE\nWITH d AS (SELECT *, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY email ORDER BY id) AS rn FROM contacts)\nDELETE FROM d WHERE rn &gt; 1;</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>After cleaning, add the constraint that should have been there: <code>CREATE UNIQUE INDEX ux_contacts_email ON contacts (lower(email));</code></p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you delete duplicate rows but keep one?</summary><div><p>Number the rows within each duplicate group with <code>ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY &lt;dup columns&gt; ORDER BY id)</code> and delete those with <code>rn &gt; 1</code>; or delete where <code>id NOT IN (SELECT MIN(id) … GROUP BY &lt;dup columns&gt;)</code>. If the table has no unique id at all, copy distinct rows to a new table (or use the engine's physical row id: <code>rowid</code> in SQLite, <code>ctid</code> in PostgreSQL).</p></div></details></section>"}